===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[subject]]; a [[theme]]; a category or general area of interest.
#: {{syn|en|ground}}
#: {{ux|en|A society where a '''topic''' cannot be discussed, does not have free speech.}}
#: {{ux|en|stick to the '''topic'''}}
#: {{ux|en|an interesting '''topic''' of conversation}}
#: {{ux|en|romance is a '''topic''' that frequently comes up in conversation}}
#* {{RQ:Economist|date=2013-08-03|volume=408|issue=8847|title=The machine of a new soul|url=http://www.economist.com/news/science-and-technology/21582495-computers-will-help-people-understand-brains-better-and-understanding-brains|passage=The yawning gap in neuroscientists’ understanding of their '''topic''' is in the intermediate scale of the brain’s anatomy.}}
# {{lb|en|linguistics}} The entity or idea that a [[clause]] or [[sentence]] is about.
# {{lb|en|Internet}} A [[discussion]] [[thread]].
#* {{quote-web|en|date=2026-02-11|publisher=Ice-Pick Lodge|title=Hotfix #11|site=Pathologic 3 Events|platform=Steam Community|url=https://steamcommunity.com/games/3199650/announcements/detail/540004614974472247|archiveurl=https://archive.ph/Z566z|archivedate=2026-02-19|text=If after the update you still run into bugs or odd behavior, please report them in the appropriate Steam Community thread or the relevant Discord channel/'''topic'''.}}
# {{lb|en|computing}} A [[component]] similar to a [[message queue]] which supports [[multiple]] [[subscriber]]s. 
# {{lb|en|music}} A [[musical]] [[sign]] intended to suggest a particular [[style]] or [[genre]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2012|author=Esti Sheinberg|title=Music Semiotics|page=9|passage=In Peircean terms, '''topics''' are interpretants: signifieds that become new signifiers in the endless semiotic chain of interpretations.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} An [[argument]] or [[reason]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1675|author=w:John Wilkins|title=Of the Principle and Duties of Natural Religion|passage=contumacious persons, who are not to be fixed by any principles, whom no '''topics''' can work upon}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|medicine}} An external local application or remedy, such as a plaster, a blister, etc.
#* {{RQ:Burton Melancholy|edition= 2nd|passage= Amongst '''topics''' or outward medicines none are more precious than baths.}}

====Usage notes====
* In English, topics are usually identified by [[word order]], [[intonation]] and context, rather than by a dedicated grammatical marker. A topic is often clause-initial, but may occur elsewhere.

